# .cu2-sanitize-allow — narrow, documented exceptions for cu2_sanitize_scan.py
#
# Copy to the ROOT of the repo being scanned (not into tools/sanitize/) and
# rename to `.cu2-sanitize-allow`.
#
# RULES OF THE ROAD
#   - Every entry needs a comment saying WHY. An uncommented entry is a bypass.
#   - The gate rejects broad patterns (`.*`, `.+`, bare fragments under 4 chars)
#     and unanchored `path:` entries. That rejection FAILS the run — you cannot
#     quietly widen the net.
#   - An allowlist entry means "this specific value is safe to publish", not
#     "this rule is noisy". If a rule is genuinely noisy, fix the rule.
#
# SYNTAX
#   line:<regex>   suppress any LINE matching the regex
#   path:<regex>   skip an entire FILE whose repo-relative path matches
#                  (must start with ^ or contain /)
#   <regex>        bare entries are treated as `line:`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Customer-facing hosts. These are published on marketing material and in DNS;
# naming them discloses nothing. Every OTHER *.cu-2.com host is internal
# surface area and must stay flagged.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
line:redvisor\.cu-2\.com
line:studio\.cu-2\.com
line:brand\.cu-2\.com
line:qc\.cu-2\.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Examples to copy, commented out. Uncomment and adapt as needed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public GitHub org path appears in install instructions we intend to publish.
# line:github\.com/<org>/<public-repo-name>

# Synthetic fixtures reviewed by <name> on <date>; all values are generated.
# path:^tests/fixtures/synthetic/

# Vendor documentation excerpt quoting their own public example endpoint.
# line:contoso\.vault\.azure\.net
